orm you that he was  brought to this prison in a short time  after me  here claypool writes about his friend  joseph ashburn  betsy ross's second husband he died in  the night  of the third of march after an illness  of about 10 days  which he bore with a amazing fortitude  one year later ross and claypool married  what is it like to hold that it's  completely mind blowing  this is probably only the third or  fourth time i've held it since we found  it  because we just didn't want to disrupt  debt we wanted to make sure it stayed in  great shape  eileen reached out to the revolution  museum in philadelphia which has only  been around since 2017.  she was put in contact with chief  historian philip mead  the same man who helped her cousin a  year earlier  well he said you know we have the john  claypool chess and he said i know that  side of the family i would love to  connect you and i was like that's so  amazing we would love to be connected  with them  how exciting is this to to get a find  like this and to reconnect families  together  it's one of the events of a lifetime we  had just  recently acquired this sea chest  that belonged to john claypool it was  just too good to be true  how valuable is a diary like this  it's invaluable to scholarship to the  public  it's our closest approximation of  actually meeting john claypool or betsy  ross they're gone  but their words survive claypool's  legacy is made famous because he was  married to the woman known for designing  the first american flag  even though historians debate that today  there is no direct evidence of that  but what we do know about her is that  she made lots of flags which meant  a pretty specialized artisan so she was  a businesswoman  she was an entrepreneur she was a mother  she was an exceptional woman she  really represents the many many  little-known stories  of the revolutionary era a story being  told at what feels like the perfect time  would you describe this as life-changing  it's for me  it's about honoring my mom she had such  a strong love for her family and her  family's history  and having lost her last july  for me the year has been more about  honoring  her and i just i guess i would say i'm  filled with excitement to share the  diary with  people who come and have a love of  history here  because i am a firm believer that  history always repeats itself  and if you learn from it maybe you make  better decisions going forward  for cbs this morning saturday jericho  duncan  philadelphia don't throw out what's in  the shoebox in the garage  sorry i had to say i unfortunately have  done it many times but it's so good to  know that they  they they sifted through it and found a  treasure yeah  we all can share very cool you got a  great background there dana by the way  can you  take that tight shot look at that very  patriotic very patriotic beautiful what  an american girl

